<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel I Komnenos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1118">1118</a>–<a href="/years/1180">1180</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Manuel I Comnenus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komnenian-dynasty">Komnenian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel I Komnenos was Byzantine emperor from 1143 to 1180. He pursued ambitious policies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> in 1167 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> under Byzantine control. He raised the Hungarian prince Béla, the future Béla III, as his intended heir, though a heavy defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> at Myriokephalon in 1176 clouded his last years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos.jpg" alt="Manuel I Komnenos" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uploader: Tokle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_I_Comnenus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel was born on 28 November 1118 in the Purple Chamber of the Great Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and was therefore called Porphyrogenitus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/born-in-the-purple">born in the purple</a>. He was the eighth child and fourth son of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a> and Irene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He was trained as a soldier and accompanied his father on campaigns against the Anatolian Turks. In 1143 John lay dying from a hunting accident and chose Manuel over his elder surviving brother Isaac, citing Manuel's courage and readiness to take advice. After John died on 8 April 1143 the armies acclaimed Manuel, and he was crowned in Constantinople in mid-August 1143 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-axouch">John Axouch</a> had arrested Isaac.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Manuel pursued an energetic foreign policy meant to restore the empire as the great power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. In 1146 he led a punitive expedition against Masud, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Rûm. In 1147 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> crossed his lands, with clashes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and Germans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> captured Corfu. Manuel allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III of Germany</a> and the Venetians and recovered Corfu in 1149. After Conrad died in 1152 no agreement could be reached with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, and Manuel turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the crusader states.</p><p>In 1167 an army under Andronikos Kontostephanos won a decisive victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, and the peace that followed gave the empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, so that by 1168 nearly the whole eastern Adriatic coast was in his hands. He established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the crusader states, and in autumn 1169 sent a large fleet and army to join King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a> in an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Manuel's last years were marked by the defeat at Myriokephalon, which resulted largely from his attacking a well-defended Seljuk position. He then made an advantageous peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a>, but Myriokephalon proved the empire's last unsuccessful effort to recover the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. He died on 24 September 1180, and his son Alexios II Komnenos succeeded him.</p><p>His secretary John Kinnamos wrote a history that credits him with every virtue. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates">Niketas Choniates</a> criticised him for raising taxes and spending lavishly, some of it on failed expeditions. Some historians hold that the power he wielded belonged to the Komnenos dynasty rather than to him, and others look to his reign for the causes of the empire's steep decline after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel I Komnenos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pecheneg troops still fought for Byzantium: at Otranto in 1105 or 1106 against Bohemond, and for <b>Manuel I Komnenos</b> in southern Italy against William the Bad, some of them at the Battle of Andria in 1155.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1176 Kilij Arslan II trapped and defeated the army of the Byzantine emperor <b>Manuel I Komnenos</b> at the Battle of Myriokephalon, ending Byzantine hopes of recovering central Anatolia; two years later he absorbed the rival Danishmendid emirate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>John Tzetzes wrote Homeric Allegories for the consort of <b>Manuel I Komnenos</b>, and Eustathios of Thessalonike wrote commentaries later generations treated as authoritative.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Freedom came by manumission, and under Justinian’s law a freed slave became a citizen; <b>Manuel I Komnenos</b> freed all the slaves of Constantinople in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Having received the submission of Raynald of Châtillon, Prince of Antioch, the Byzantine emperor <b>Manuel I Komnenos</b> rode through Antioch in a grand ceremonial procession.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine army of <b>Manuel I Komnenos</b>, under Andronikos Kontostephanos, decisively defeated the Hungarians near Sirmium, forcing them to sue for peace on Byzantine terms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-8">8 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1158" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manuel I Komnenos"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1158">What was here in 1158? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
